::PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
Which statement correctly uses polynomial theorems?
{
=A. If f(c)=0, then x-c is a factor.#Correct. The Factor Theorem connects f(c)=0 with factor x-c.
~B. If f(c)=0, then x+c is always a factor.#This choice reflects a common misconception. Use the model type to choose the correct rule.
~C. A remainder is never connected to f(c).#This choice is not supported by the function evidence. Connect the answer back to the model.
~D. Factors cannot identify zeros.#
}
::PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
Which feature helps graph a polynomial of degree 3 or higher?
{
~A. Only the y-axis label#This choice does not match the lesson concept. Recheck the feature or restriction being tested.
=B. Zeros and end behavior#A label alone does not show polynomial behavior.
~C. Only the table title#This choice is not supported by the function evidence. Connect the answer back to the model.
~D. The graph color#
}
::PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.3
Question:
What should be checked when interpreting a polynomial model in context?
{
~A. Only the largest exponent#This choice does not match the lesson concept. Recheck the feature or restriction being tested.
~B. Only all real numbers#This choice reflects a common misconception. Use the model type to choose the correct rule.
=C. Constraints and key features#Degree matters, but it is not the whole interpretation.
~D. Only the calculator window#
}
::PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
What must be excluded in a rational function model?
{
~A. Every decimal input#This choice does not match the lesson concept. Recheck the feature or restriction being tested.
~B. Inputs that make the numerator positive#This choice reflects a common misconception. Use the model type to choose the correct rule.
~C. Every negative input#This choice is not supported by the function evidence. Connect the answer back to the model.
=D. Inputs that make the denominator 0#Decimal inputs may be allowed.
}
::PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which statement correctly describes an exponential growth model?
{
=A. The base is greater than 1.#Correct. A base greater than 1 shows repeated growth.
~B. The base is between 0 and 1.#This choice reflects a common misconception. Use the model type to choose the correct rule.
~C. The variable is never in the exponent.#This choice is not supported by the function evidence. Connect the answer back to the model.
~D. The initial value must be 0.#
}
::PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q06::
Question ID: PC_U02_UNIT_ASSESS_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
Which statement correctly describes logarithmic meaning?
{
~A. A logarithm argument can be 0.#This choice does not match the lesson concept. Recheck the feature or restriction being tested.
=B. A logarithm asks for an exponent.#A logarithm argument must be positive.
~C. A logarithm base can be 1.#This choice is not supported by the function evidence. Connect the answer back to the model.
~D. Logarithmic models have no domain restrictions.#
}
